</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Distribution of attention scores in Dream-7B and MMaDA-8B </figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional plots</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A0.F9" title="In Attention Sinks in Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> we show additional plots of attention score distribution, displaying how a only a few tokens, the sinks, receive a disproportionate high attention score.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Selection of Sink Threshold</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 4.1 Definition of Attention Sink ‣ 4 Analysis of Attention Sinks in Masked Diffusion Language Models ‣ Attention Sinks in Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we defined <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to be the threshold for classifying a token as a sink. In <a href="#A2.F10" title="In Appendix B Selection of Sink Threshold ‣ Attention Sinks in Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> we show how the value of <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> affects sink selection. We see that most all the analysed DLMs filter out at least 96% of tokens when usigng <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15731v1/avg_64_epsilon.svg" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/416;" width="476" height="416"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Percentage of tokens selected when increasing the value of <math id="A2.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, for a sequence of 64 tokens. A balanced threshold is found at <math id="A2.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=3</annotation></semantics></math>, which we used in this investigation to define that a token is a sink.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
